/* std/irq: an interrupt line as a capability (kernel stage 18, MK10-S1's
   second half, `plan/irq-frame-caps.md`).

   An interrupt line is a resource a driver must be granted, and the grant is a
   *value*: `claim` is the only way a `Line` enters a program, and the compiler
   checks the argument against the calling module's `irq` grant at the call. A
   constant line has to fall inside a granted range, and a computed one is
   refused unless the grant is the whole-line `["*"]`, exactly as the `mmio`
   address check treats a `Ptr.from_addr` (`plan/device-caps.md`).

       datac Line(val n: u32)

       fun claim(n: u32).return: Line
       fun Line.number().return: u32

   The field is not exported, so a `Line` cannot be built outside this module:
   a struct literal from another module is refused (`stdlib.rs::SEALED_TYPES`),
   and `claim` is the one constructor. The module is a capability domain
   (`stdlib::domain_of` maps it to `irq`), so every item here needs the domain
   too: a module that holds a `Line` but no `irq` grant can carry it but not
   read it, which is what makes the grant, rather than possession alone, the
   authority.

   A hosted program may import this module and claim a line as well: the check
   is a compile-time one and has nothing to do with the machine, but a hosted
   program has no interrupt hardware, so a line it "claims" is only the number
   the compiler admitted. Freestanding is where the number names something.

   Undefined behaviour this does not remove: a line number is a number, and
   nothing ties it to a real interrupt controller, to a mask bit or to a
   handler that is actually installed. `plan/kernel-ub-notes.md` stage 18 says
   so. */

datac Line(val n: u32)

// The receipt for an `irq` grant: a line the calling module was granted.
fun claim(n: u32).return: Line = Line(n = n)

// The line's number.
fun Line.number().return: u32 = n
